.TH LOD.QOS_WRR_ENABLE 4 2026-10-02 Lustre "Lustre Kernel Interfaces"
.SH NAME
lod.qos_wrr_enable \- allocate targets by weighted round-robin
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY
.BI "lctl get_param lod." FSNAME "*.qos_wrr_enable"
.SY
.BI "lctl set_param lod." FSNAME "*.qos_wrr_enable=" 0|1
.YS
.SS PROPERTIES
.TP
.B Perms
.br
.BR 644 " | " -rw-r--r--
.TP
.B Scope
.br
Per-MDT
.TP
.B Config
.br
Always present on MDS nodes
.TP
.B Default
.br
.RB lod.*.qos_wrr_enable= 1
.TP
.B Valid Range
.br
.RB lod.*.qos_wrr_enable= 0
.br
.RB lod.*.qos_wrr_enable= 1
.SH DESCRIPTION
When the free space of the OSTs differs by more than
.BR lod.qos_threshold_rr (4),
the MDS allocates new OST objects by the free space of each OST, weighted
as described in
.BR lod.qos_prio_free (4).
With
.B qos_wrr_enable
set to 1, the OSTs are used in round-robin order, each in proportion to its
weight.
With 0, each stripe is put on an OST picked at random in proportion to the
same weights as weighted round-robin allocation, without the penalties
for recently used OSTs and OSSs that were used before release 2.18.0.
This is a fallback in case weighted round-robin allocation causes problems,
and makes the balance worse over short runs.
.SH EXAMPLES
Allocate OST objects at random by weight on all MDTs of the
.B testfs
filesystem:
.EX
.RB "mds# " "lctl set_param lod.testfs-*.qos_wrr_enable=0"
.EE
.SH AVAILABILITY
.B qos_wrr_enable
is part of the
.BR lustre (7)
filesystem package since release 2.18.0.
.\" Added in commit 2.17.59
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lod.qos_ost_weights (4),
.BR lod.qos_prio_free (4),
.BR lod.qos_threshold_rr (4),
.BR lctl-get_param (8),
.BR lctl-set_param (8)
